    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A city-state is a city that governs itself and the countryside around it as a state of its own, with its own gods, laws and army and often citizens who share in government.</li><li>City-states arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, among the Maya, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast and in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, usually in clusters of rivals sharing one culture.</li><li>Small size fostered participation, trade and invention but left city-states open to faction and conquest; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, Monaco and Vatican City are their sovereign heirs today.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s school collected the constitutions of 158 states, but only the Constitution of the Athenians survives, recovered from papyri found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and first published in 1891.</p></aside>
    </section>

