    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polis.jpg" alt="Polis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polis">Polis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attica_06-13_Athens_50_View_from_Philopappos_-_Acropolis_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A.Savin, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A city-state is a city that governs itself together with the countryside around it, so that the urban centre and the state coincide. The Danish historian Mogens Herman Hansen, who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">Polis</a> Centre’s comparison of some thirty city-state cultures, defined it as “a self-governing, but not necessarily independent, political unit”: many city-states acknowledged an overlord or belonged to a league while running their own affairs. Most were small, a walled town with its farmland and a few villages, and they usually arose in clusters, what Hansen called city-state cultures: neighbours that shared a language, religion and way of life but fought, allied and traded with one another as separate states.</p><p>A city-state could have almost any constitution. Sumerian cities were ruled by lords and kings who governed in the name of the city’s god; Phoenician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> by kings with councils of elders; the Classic Maya cities by holy lords, k’uhul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajaw">ajaw</a>. Greek poleis ranged from narrow oligarchies to the democracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where an assembly open to all citizens, a Council of Five Hundred chosen by lot and popular courts governed. Medieval Italian communes were run by elected consuls and councils and, from the late 12th century, often by a podestà, a chief magistrate hired from another city for a fixed term so that he would stand above local factions. Hanseatic towns were governed by councils of leading merchants, many under the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>.</p><p>What set the city-state apart was its scale and the close bond between the state and its people. Citizenship, where it existed, was hereditary and exclusive: in Athens from 451 BCE only the children of two Athenian parents were citizens, and women, slaves and resident foreigners had no share in politics. Citizens usually defended the city themselves, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> or militias in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and its laws, gods and festivals defined who they were. A city-state differs from a territorial kingdom or empire, which rules many cities from one capital, and from a league or federation, in which cities pool defence and diplomacy: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> had no permanent administration, treasury or army, and each member town kept its own council and laws.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures.jpg" alt="Stele of the Vultures" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_the_Vultures_in_the_Louvre_Museum_(enhanced_composite).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Background: Kikuyu3 Elements: Eric Gaba (Sting) Composite: पाटलिपुत्र 10:52, 30 April 2020 (UTC), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first cities and the first city-states arose together in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. In the 4th millennium BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> grew around the temple precincts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eanna">Eanna</a> and of the sky god An into the largest settlement of its age, and towards the end of the millennium its scribes produced the earliest known writing, mostly administrative accounts. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a> (c. 2900–2350 BCE) a dozen or more independent cities, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> and Uruk itself, shared the plain. Each belonged in theory to its god, whose temple held much of the land, and was ruled by a lord, en or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">ensi</a>, or a king, lugal, literally “big man”. Thorkild Jacobsen argued in 1943 that assemblies had once decided war and peace, citing the Sumerian poem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aga-of-kish">Aga of Kish</a>, in which Gilgamesh consults the elders and then the young men of Uruk before defying Kish.</p><p>The cities fought over water and fields. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a>, raised about 2450 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum of Lagash</a>, celebrates his victory over neighbouring Umma in a long dispute over the land between them, and around 2350 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urukagina">Urukagina</a> of Lagash proclaimed reforms against the exactions of officials and priests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugal-zage-si">Lugal-zage-si</a> of Umma then conquered Lagash and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> defeated him and brought the cities under one king. Under the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, Ur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> the old cities became provinces, though they kept their gods, temples and local assemblies.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> gave the form its classic name and its first theory. The polis, which took shape from the 8th century BCE, was both a community of citizens and a state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s Politics declares that “man is by nature a political animal”, defines the polis as a community of citizens and compares the constitutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, and his school collected the constitutions of 158 states; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>’s Laws planned an ideal city of 5,040 households. The modern term is younger. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Johann Gottfried Herder</a> applied the German Stadtstaat to the polis in 1765, and the English historian W. Warde Fowler introduced “city-state” in 1893 for “a state in which the whole life and energy of the people, political, intellectual, religious, is focused at one point, and that point a city”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnano"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnano.jpg" alt="Battle of Legnano" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnano">Battle of Legnano</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BattagliaLegnano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Faberh at Italian Wikipedia., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the late 2nd millennium BCE the Phoenician cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and founded colonies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, founded by Tyre according to tradition in 814 BCE, became a great power, governed by two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffete">suffetes</a> elected each year, a council of elders and a popular assembly. Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a> multiplied from the 8th century BCE and planted colonies from Marseille to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>; the inventory compiled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> Polis Centre lists more than a thousand. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> led the Greek resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 480–479 BCE and then fought each other in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> (431–404 BCE). After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> defeated Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> at Chaeronea in 338 BCE, the poleis lived on as self-governing cities under Hellenistic kings and then under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which had itself grown from a city-state into an empire.</p><p>Similar clusters appeared far from the Mediterranean. In the Classic Maya lowlands (c. 250–900 CE) dozens of cities such as Tikal, Calakmul, Copán and Palenque were ruled by holy lords who recorded their wars and alliances in inscriptions; Tikal and Calakmul led rival networks of allies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and in 562 Tikal was defeated by Caracol, an ally of Calakmul. Most cities of the southern lowlands were abandoned in the 9th century. On the East African coast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> city-states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani">Kilwa Kisiwani</a>, Mombasa, Malindi and Pate, Muslim merchant towns under their own rulers, grew rich on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited in 1331, praised Kilwa as one of the most beautiful towns in the world. In 1505 a Portuguese fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> sacked Kilwa and Mombasa.</p><p>In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the communes of the 11th and 12th centuries took over the powers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and counts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombard-league">Lombard League</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnano">Legnano</a> in 1176, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-constance">Peace of Constance</a> of 1183 recognised the cities’ right to elect their own consuls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> became maritime powers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> a centre of banking and cloth; by 1300 most communes had fallen under a signore, but Venice, Florence, Genoa and Lucca remained republics. In the north, German trading towns led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, whose victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a>, sealed by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> in 1370, marked the height of its power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">Free imperial cities</a>, the Novgorod Republic, the Republic of Ragusa and the mueang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> were city-states too, but territorial states absorbed most of them between the 15th and 19th centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/liber-abaci"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liber-abaci.jpg" alt="Liber Abaci" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/liber-abaci">Liber Abaci</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liber_abbaci_magliab_f124r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Political thinkers have long tied self-government to small size. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> defined the citizen as one who shares in judging and ruling, and held that a city must be small enough for its citizens to know one another’s characters if they are to choose officials and judge disputes well. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> argued in The Spirit of the Laws (1748) that it is natural for a republic to have only a small territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>, who signed himself a citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, held in The Social Contract (1762) that a people could make its own laws only in a state small enough for it to assemble. Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where thousands of citizens attended the assembly and served on the council and in the courts, was their great example.</p><p>City-states have also been credited with remarkable economic and cultural creativity. Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> became the most urban and commercial region of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the chronicler Giovanni Villani reckoned that some 8,000 to 10,000 Florentine children were learning to read around 1338. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fibonacci">Leonardo Fibonacci</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liber-abaci">Liber Abaci</a> (1202) taught merchants Hindu-Arabic arithmetic, Italian merchants developed double-entry bookkeeping, set out in print by Luca Pacioli in 1494, and Florentine humanists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a> praised their republic’s liberty as the source of its greatness, an outlook the historian Hans Baron called civic humanism. Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, both city-states, gave Europe much of what it later counted as its classical inheritance.</p><p>Defenders also point to the stability and cooperation some city-states achieved. Aristotle praised the constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, noting that it had suffered no rebellion worth mentioning and no tyrant. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> kept its institutions, from the elected doge to the Great Council, for five centuries after 1297, and writers such as Gasparo Contarini held it up as a model of balanced mixed government. The Hanseatic towns, with no common government, settled their affairs by consensus at their diets, spread a common town law from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and for three centuries kept the trade of the Baltic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> largely in their hands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527.jpg" alt="Sack of Rome (1527)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome (1527)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sack_of_Rome_of_1527_by_Johannes_Lingelbach_17th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johannes Lingelbach, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The commonest charge against the city-state is weakness. A single city could rarely resist a territorial kingdom: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> absorbed the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a>, and the French and Spanish invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1494 left only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and a few smaller republics independent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a>, written in 1513, with a plea for a prince to free Italy from the “barbarians”, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527 showed how exposed the divided peninsula had become. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> towns could not resist Portuguese warships in 1505, and the Hanseatic towns fell one by one under the authority of princes and kings.</p><p>Faction was the second charge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> described how civil war at Corcyra in 427 BCE set democrats and oligarchs to murdering one another, and Greek cities repeatedly called in outside powers against their own citizens. Italian communes split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelphs-and-ghibellines">Guelphs and Ghibellines</a> and between rival families: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a> was exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1302 when his party lost, and the Florentine wool workers rose in the Ciompi revolt of 1378. In The Federalist No. 9 (1787) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a> wrote that “it is impossible to read the history of the petty republics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and Italy without feeling sensations of horror and disgust at the distractions with which they were continually agitated”, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> argued in No. 10 that a large republic would control faction better than a small one.</p><p>Critics also note how narrow city-state freedom was. Athenian democracy excluded women, foreigners and tens of thousands of slaves, and the closing of Venice’s Great Council in 1297 made government the hereditary preserve of a closed patriciate. City-states that lived by trade were exposed when trade moved: the Hanseatic towns’ privileges abroad provoked resentment, which their refusal to grant others the same rights hardened, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> closed their office at Novgorod in 1494 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> expelled them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1597, while new Atlantic and Cape routes drew commerce away from both the Hanse and Italy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli.jpg" alt="Niccolò Machiavelli" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Niccol%C3%B2_Machiavelli_by_Santi_di_Tito.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Santi di Tito, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city-state left political thought much of its vocabulary and its ideals. Politics, policy, police and polity all descend from polis and its derivatives, and the ideas of citizenship, civic duty and republican liberty were worked out in city-states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/discourses-on-livy">Discourses on Livy</a>, drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, shaped a republican tradition that passed through James Harrington’s Oceana (1656) to the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, even as they rejected the small republic itself.</p><p>Many city-states survived into the modern age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> were sovereign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> in the 19th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> a free city from 1815 to 1846; Danzig was a free city under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> from 1920 to 1939 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> a free territory from 1947 to 1954. Hamburg and Bremen, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, are still city-states within the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and Hamburg and Bremen keep the title of free Hanseatic city. Three sovereign city-states remain. Vatican City was created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> of 1929; Monaco has been ruled by the Grimaldi family since 1297, with brief interruptions; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, which left the Federation of Malaysia in 1965, has been called by The Economist the “world’s only fully functioning city-state”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, special administrative regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, are often described as city-states without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><p>Historians still debate the form. Max Weber’s essay The City (1921) treated the self-governing commune of citizens as peculiar to the West; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> comparisons, which found city-state cultures from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, show that the form arose independently in several parts of the world. Specialists also dispute how far the term fits: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> as a polis made up of villages, and Hansen’s team found many dependent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a> that were not sovereign. Most now see city-state cultures as among history’s most creative political settings and among its most fragile, flourishing where no larger power could unite a region and fading when one did.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The city-state, a city that governs itself and its countryside, was among the earliest forms of state and one of the most inventive. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to Kilwa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> it fostered citizenship, commerce and republican thought, though its small size left it prone to faction and conquest; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, Monaco and Vatican City carry it on today.</em></p>

